I was about to say that using an attitudinal for sarcasm, irony, and/or=20
joking would sort of ruin the intended effect -- but actually I think there=
=20
*is* an appropriate use case: informal text.

Attitudinals are often compared to emoticons, and emoticons are commonly=20
used to help clarify the writer's intended mood and meaning in Internet=20
chat, forums, and emails. People tend to write in a very loose,=20
conversational style on the internet, so their writing lacks both the=20
precision of more formal writing and the nuance of spoken inflections and=
=20
tone. So, while announcing that you are telling a silly joke probably makes=
=20
it less funny in speech, it's maybe safer, when being ironical/sarcastic on=
=20
the internet, to append the line with a winky face, or a "jk" ("just=20
kidding"). And an attitudinal would serve the same purpose.

~Andrew


